WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.189 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  a train squeezes through this 500 meter  stretch of railway just west of bangkok  about six times a day  and every time hundreds of shopkeepers  who set up a market directly on the  tracks have to put away their tents  produce and merchandise to make way  for locals it's business as usual at the  mike long railway market even when the  train is so close  tourism is picking up again as thailand  eases its covet 19 entry restrictions  more people and money is a welcome  change  the pandemic led to a huge drop in  customers it was drastic those who  visited bought less than usual so i only  managed to make a third of what i made  before covet 19.  before the pandemic thailand saw nearly  40 million visitors a year now the  government is hoping for 15 million  despite the drop tourism can bring in  roughly 18 billion dollars into the  economy  to attract more people the market has  safety measures in place  when we sound the horn the vendors know  they must clear their stalls off the  rail track everyone is safe because the  train slows down so it's not coming at a  full speed it cruises through the market  slowly  from inside the train it's locals who  are cruising through seeing how  foreigners once again flock to the  market  it's been around since 1905. shopkeepers  say tradition is part of the attraction  years ago vendors only use umbrellas not  tents there was no electricity or water  in the market then more people started  to visit so the number of vendors also  increased  as the government tries to rebuild its  tourism industry leaders hope foot  traffic in this market will be part of a  wider trend across thailand  katya lopes  al jazeera

